{"connection_id": "BC-b053a02769a58521ad44", "ref": "1 Clement 55:2", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "self-sale to feed others", "note": "Clement recalls believers who sold themselves into slavery and used the price to feed others; this is an extreme concrete instance of preferring another’s need over one’s own freedom."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae5275fc0b6937e5fecf", "ref": "2 Clement 5:1", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brief fleshly sojourn / coming kingdom", "note": "The homily calls the flesh's stay in this world brief and transient and sets it beside Christ's promised coming kingdom and eternal life, closely echoing the commentary's contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6eec0231292c4a4070a2", "ref": "2 Clement 6:1-7", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "this world and the next as opposed masters", "note": "The homily says the present and coming worlds are enemies and asks what profit is gaining the whole world while losing one’s soul; it turns the commentary’s choice into an explicit refusal to serve both."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc93c38fd544ce700c8f", "ref": "Augustine, On Christian Doctrine 1.4.4", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "things to use and things to enjoy", "note": "Augustine distinguishes temporary things used on the way from God, who is to be enjoyed as beatitude; this interpretive hierarchy clarifies what a rightly ordered preference can mean."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f569952be13a422fa2db", "ref": "Augustine, Sermon 103", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Martha’s present service and Mary’s lasting portion", "note": "Augustine reads Martha’s activity as the present life and Mary’s contemplation as the life to come; this interprets Luke’s portion “not taken away” as the permanent good over the present age."}
{"connection_id": "BC-803a7be574ca17912507", "ref": "Didache 1:4-5", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "give without seeking return", "note": "The teaching commands love for enemies and giving without demanding repayment; it turns the commentary’s preference for another over self into a specific Christian practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c681ad1b349dcc57211e", "ref": "Didache 4:8", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sharing perishable goods because of imperishable goods", "note": "The Two Ways instruction requires sharing with a needy brother and argues that participants in imperishable things should share perishable things all the more; it unites other-preference with a hierarchy of goods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a55d7e2c5a1f4b8f4fa1", "ref": "Gospel of Thomas 56", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "world called a corpse", "note": "Thomas says one who knows the world has found a corpse and that the world is not worthy of that person; this is a specific, radical devaluation of the world alongside the commentary’s low/dregs imagery, though it does not itself state an afterlife contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11636f2b5bdd0726a7ba", "ref": "John Chrysostom, Homilies on Matthew 20", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "heavenly treasure through almsgiving", "note": "Chrysostom reads Jesus’ contrast between earthly and heavenly treasure as a call to give wealth to the poor; this makes the commentary’s choice of the better good an ethical use of possessions."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8699595304f70c3c7b65", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.1.28", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "God chooses low and despised", "note": "God chooses what the world calls low and despised to nullify what is; this directly counters an inference that social lowliness marks what should be discarded."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1c45991ed222462f62d8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.24", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "not one's own interest", "note": "The instruction not to seek one's own interest but that of the other gives a compact analogue to the commentary's positive sense of ithar."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d3fd5d30e4894bbf27a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.12", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gold beside hay and straw", "note": "Paul contrasts durable building materials with wood, hay, and straw; this gives the commentary’s distinction between selected quality and debris a testable image of works."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5ca24da49b2dfdfeed1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.12", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "durable materials / wood, hay, straw", "note": "Builders use either gold, silver, and precious stones or wood, hay, and straw, a material hierarchy that gives the commentary's selected-versus-dross image a concrete test case."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7af0f65bd40b3388f252", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work tested by fire", "note": "Each person’s work is revealed and tested by fire; beside 87:5’s debris and 87:17’s better lasting lot, the verse makes the sorting depend on what endures the test."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7722a8555b6d35a82fdf", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fire testing each work's quality", "note": "The day exposes each person's work and fire tests its quality, so apparent building material is sorted by what endures testing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-69aa1dcdf92c8861a21b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "world passes, God’s will remains", "note": "The world and its desires pass away, but the one doing God’s will abides forever; this directly pairs 87:16’s worldly life with 87:17’s lasting alternative."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70b260e430d04cab4090", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "passing world / remaining doer", "note": "The world and its desire pass away, while the doer of God's will remains forever; it closely matches the rejected worldly preference and abiding good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-99d1fe44db74895f1db5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flesh as grass", "note": "Peter quotes Isaiah’s grass and flower fading to describe all flesh; this brings the commentary’s pasture/debris image into an explicit contrast with permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8606482f85af6ec4073b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "flesh and glory as grass", "note": "Peter reapplies Isaiah's grass image to all flesh and human glory: grass withers and its flower falls. This makes transient human status explicit beside the commentary's grass and lowly-human imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7815f2f4225fd6c36187", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah’s word applied to gospel", "note": "Peter applies Isaiah’s enduring word to the good news preached to his hearers; this Christian reading makes the commentary’s recitation in 87:6 an enduring proclamation amid fading life."}

