{"connection_id": "BC-1c45991ed222462f62d8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.10.24", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "not one's own interest", "note": "The instruction not to seek one's own interest but that of the other gives a compact analogue to the commentary's positive sense of ithar."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6d3fd5d30e4894bbf27a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.12", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gold beside hay and straw", "note": "Paul contrasts durable building materials with wood, hay, and straw; this gives the commentary’s distinction between selected quality and debris a testable image of works."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5ca24da49b2dfdfeed1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.12", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "durable materials / wood, hay, straw", "note": "Builders use either gold, silver, and precious stones or wood, hay, and straw, a material hierarchy that gives the commentary's selected-versus-dross image a concrete test case."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7af0f65bd40b3388f252", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work tested by fire", "note": "Each person’s work is revealed and tested by fire; beside 87:5’s debris and 87:17’s better lasting lot, the verse makes the sorting depend on what endures the test."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7722a8555b6d35a82fdf", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.3.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fire testing each work's quality", "note": "The day exposes each person's work and fire tests its quality, so apparent building material is sorted by what endures testing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-69aa1dcdf92c8861a21b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "world passes, God’s will remains", "note": "The world and its desires pass away, but the one doing God’s will abides forever; this directly pairs 87:16’s worldly life with 87:17’s lasting alternative."}
{"connection_id": "BC-70b260e430d04cab4090", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "passing world / remaining doer", "note": "The world and its desire pass away, while the doer of God's will remains forever; it closely matches the rejected worldly preference and abiding good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-99d1fe44db74895f1db5", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flesh as grass", "note": "Peter quotes Isaiah’s grass and flower fading to describe all flesh; this brings the commentary’s pasture/debris image into an explicit contrast with permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8606482f85af6ec4073b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.24", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "flesh and glory as grass", "note": "Peter reapplies Isaiah's grass image to all flesh and human glory: grass withers and its flower falls. This makes transient human status explicit beside the commentary's grass and lowly-human imagery."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7815f2f4225fd6c36187", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah’s word applied to gospel", "note": "Peter applies Isaiah’s enduring word to the good news preached to his hearers; this Christian reading makes the commentary’s recitation in 87:6 an enduring proclamation amid fading life."}

