{"connection_id": "BC-df9340f83a131a7dd687", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.25", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "enduring word after grass", "note": "Peter's continuation says the Lord's word remains forever and identifies it with the preached good news, interpreting the fading-grass contrast as a durable divine message."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c559d2d4179dc5d50bea", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God alone has immortality", "note": "God alone possesses immortality and dwells in unapproachable light, closely corresponding to the commentary's statement that enduring being belongs uniquely to God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8477dc17bad6b1c9b4e8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hope not in uncertain riches", "note": "Paul tells the rich not to set hope on uncertain wealth but on God; this sharpens the commentary’s question of which goods deserve to be preferred."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e86e2b7682a0c90e90e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "uncertain wealth / hope in God", "note": "The rich are told not to set hope on uncertain wealth but on God, turning the commentary's hierarchy of goods into an instruction about trust."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d511e5f945d72ad336f8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure for the future", "note": "Rich believers are to store a good foundation for the future and take hold of real life; this connects wealth rightly used with a more enduring life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1976737ca7a8643d808", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "good foundation for the future", "note": "Generosity stores a good foundation for the future and takes hold of true life, connecting cleanly directed wealth, self-giving, and an enduring good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37b1b2273156ca6eb98b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.6", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "contentment as great gain", "note": "Paul calls godliness with contentment great gain; this counters a material reading of “good” wealth by locating true gain in a moral disposition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-40c7e3cb76f4947682ce", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "outer wasting, inner renewal", "note": "Paul says the outer person is wasting away while the inner person is renewed day by day; this adds an inward, present renewal to the commentary’s contrast between decaying debris and abiding good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28c5962adc3f0fddfd55", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seen temporary, unseen eternal", "note": "Paul contrasts visible things that are temporary with unseen things that are eternal; this states the temporal/permanent valuation underlying 87:16–17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ccdfe1c62bc5520eb46", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seen temporary / unseen eternal", "note": "Paul distinguishes temporary visible things from eternal unseen things, supplying the same evaluative contrast that governs the two sides of the commentary's scale."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3674d099d9b93862ef5", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.1", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earthly tent / eternal house", "note": "When the earthly tent is destroyed, believers have an eternal, God-made house in heaven; the image makes earthly dissolution and lasting dwelling a direct pair."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b2b158feaf68d334e8b2", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.9.9", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "giving to poor / righteousness remaining", "note": "The generous person scatters gifts to the poor and his righteousness remains forever, directly uniting self-giving with the commentary's positive language of what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ac376a96585b577ef9c", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.1.9", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eternal destruction", "note": "Paul describes the fate of those who reject God as eternal destruction; this is a contrary lasting outcome to the good and abiding afterlife in 87:17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1b72cf63fb0abd8e39f", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.20", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "honorable and dishonorable vessels", "note": "A household contains gold and silver vessels as well as wooden and earthen ones, some for honor and some for dishonor; this is a concrete image of differentiated worth rather than a shared story."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd9d0096107f3a2bfb30", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.21", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cleansed vessel for honorable use", "note": "The one who cleanses himself becomes a vessel set apart for honorable use; this adds purification to the commentary’s image of selecting what contains no dross."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b95b0425abadd73cfaa8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.35", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "giving more blessed than receiving", "note": "Paul recalls Jesus’ saying that giving is more blessed than receiving; this directly illuminates the commentary’s definition of īthār as preferring another through generosity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-24eddbc67c170fec88c7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.35", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "giving more blessed than receiving", "note": "Paul's remembered saying of Jesus calls giving more blessed than receiving, a concise valuation of self-giving alongside the commentary's positive preference of others."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78f86313405f36a14dfc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.4.35", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "goods distributed by need", "note": "The early community lays sale proceeds at the apostles’ feet and distributes to each as needed; this gives the commentary’s prefer-others sense a concrete communal practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2844dd0af6d0d7a33942", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.5.29", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "obey God rather than humans", "note": "The apostles answer threats by saying they must obey God rather than human authorities; this is a concise Christian parallel to choosing divine truth under worldly coercion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93120629be29b873f68c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.5.29", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "obeying God over humans", "note": "The apostles state that they must obey God rather than humans, a concise Christian formulation of the threatened preference enacted in the commentary's Pharaoh scene."}

