{"connection_id": "BC-8477dc17bad6b1c9b4e8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hope not in uncertain riches", "note": "Paul tells the rich not to set hope on uncertain wealth but on God; this sharpens the commentary’s question of which goods deserve to be preferred."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e86e2b7682a0c90e90e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "uncertain wealth / hope in God", "note": "The rich are told not to set hope on uncertain wealth but on God, turning the commentary's hierarchy of goods into an instruction about trust."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d511e5f945d72ad336f8", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure for the future", "note": "Rich believers are to store a good foundation for the future and take hold of real life; this connects wealth rightly used with a more enduring life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1976737ca7a8643d808", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "good foundation for the future", "note": "Generosity stores a good foundation for the future and takes hold of true life, connecting cleanly directed wealth, self-giving, and an enduring good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37b1b2273156ca6eb98b", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Tim.6.6", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "contentment as great gain", "note": "Paul calls godliness with contentment great gain; this counters a material reading of “good” wealth by locating true gain in a moral disposition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-40c7e3cb76f4947682ce", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "outer wasting, inner renewal", "note": "Paul says the outer person is wasting away while the inner person is renewed day by day; this adds an inward, present renewal to the commentary’s contrast between decaying debris and abiding good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28c5962adc3f0fddfd55", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seen temporary, unseen eternal", "note": "Paul contrasts visible things that are temporary with unseen things that are eternal; this states the temporal/permanent valuation underlying 87:16–17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9ccdfe1c62bc5520eb46", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.18", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seen temporary / unseen eternal", "note": "Paul distinguishes temporary visible things from eternal unseen things, supplying the same evaluative contrast that governs the two sides of the commentary's scale."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b3674d099d9b93862ef5", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.1", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earthly tent / eternal house", "note": "When the earthly tent is destroyed, believers have an eternal, God-made house in heaven; the image makes earthly dissolution and lasting dwelling a direct pair."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b2b158feaf68d334e8b2", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.9.9", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "giving to poor / righteousness remaining", "note": "The generous person scatters gifts to the poor and his righteousness remains forever, directly uniting self-giving with the commentary's positive language of what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4ac376a96585b577ef9c", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.1.9", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eternal destruction", "note": "Paul describes the fate of those who reject God as eternal destruction; this is a contrary lasting outcome to the good and abiding afterlife in 87:17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c1b72cf63fb0abd8e39f", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.20", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "honorable and dishonorable vessels", "note": "A household contains gold and silver vessels as well as wooden and earthen ones, some for honor and some for dishonor; this is a concrete image of differentiated worth rather than a shared story."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cd9d0096107f3a2bfb30", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Tim.2.21", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "cleansed vessel for honorable use", "note": "The one who cleanses himself becomes a vessel set apart for honorable use; this adds purification to the commentary’s image of selecting what contains no dross."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b95b0425abadd73cfaa8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.35", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "giving more blessed than receiving", "note": "Paul recalls Jesus’ saying that giving is more blessed than receiving; this directly illuminates the commentary’s definition of īthār as preferring another through generosity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-24eddbc67c170fec88c7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.20.35", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "giving more blessed than receiving", "note": "Paul's remembered saying of Jesus calls giving more blessed than receiving, a concise valuation of self-giving alongside the commentary's positive preference of others."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78f86313405f36a14dfc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.4.35", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "goods distributed by need", "note": "The early community lays sale proceeds at the apostles’ feet and distributes to each as needed; this gives the commentary’s prefer-others sense a concrete communal practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2844dd0af6d0d7a33942", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.5.29", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "obey God rather than humans", "note": "The apostles answer threats by saying they must obey God rather than human authorities; this is a concise Christian parallel to choosing divine truth under worldly coercion."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93120629be29b873f68c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.5.29", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "obeying God over humans", "note": "The apostles state that they must obey God rather than humans, a concise Christian formulation of the threatened preference enacted in the commentary's Pharaoh scene."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-00acf308717541417428", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.1", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seek the things above", "note": "Those raised with Christ are instructed to seek the things above, where Christ is, making selection of an upper and lasting good an explicit practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e3526a30ca720daede9", "ref": "SBLGNT:Col.3.2", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mind above, not earth", "note": "The command to set the mind on things above rather than things on earth is a concise counterpart to refusing to privilege dunya."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca75027f55dca5f85e64", "ref": "SBLGNT:Gal.6.8", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sowing flesh / sowing Spirit", "note": "Sowing to the flesh reaps corruption, while sowing to the Spirit reaps eternal life; the agricultural metaphor turns the commentary's two-sided choice into two opposed harvests."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1fdc9012b78bbfbda8b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.34", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "better and abiding possession", "note": "Believers accept confiscation of property because they know they have a better and abiding possession; this nearly reproduces the commentary’s contrast between worldly goods and what is better and lasting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-708081dab2e321199014", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.10.34", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "better and abiding possession", "note": "Those who lose property under pressure accept it joyfully because they know they have a better, abiding possession; this gives the commentary's better-and-more-lasting contrast a concrete setting of threatened choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-38f211d47897f082b05e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.25", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "choose hardship over sin’s pleasure", "note": "Moses chooses mistreatment with God’s people over the temporary enjoyment of sin; this makes the choice against present worldly pleasure explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-453d5921a0a70e115131", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.25", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "choosing affliction / temporary sin", "note": "Moses chooses mistreatment with God's people over temporary enjoyment of sin, a direct scriptural action of preferring a costly better good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0e1fe0cfbf51729d8a86", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.26", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "greater wealth and future reward", "note": "Moses regards reproach as greater wealth than Egypt’s treasures because he looks to the reward; the verse directly reorders worldly wealth by an enduring good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01c4473a553783c724ee", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.26", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Egyptian treasure / reward", "note": "Moses judges reproach greater wealth than Egypt's treasures because he looks to the reward; Pharaoh's sphere becomes the setting for a valuation unlike the fleeting ruler's power."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6f3b640ad1fa203077f0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.35", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refuse release for better resurrection", "note": "Some tortured witnesses refuse release so they may obtain a better resurrection; this gives preference for the afterlife a concrete cost in present life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ccaaa3a2615443034b02", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.11.35", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "refused release / better resurrection", "note": "Tortured faithful people refuse release in order to obtain a better resurrection, a direct instance of choosing costly fidelity for a superior future."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c7fea4f24dabe1e60510", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Esau sells inheritance for one meal", "note": "Hebrews calls Esau profane because he sells his birthright for one meal; it interprets the Genesis scene as a choice of immediate appetite over inheritance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4be7fcf119e65605591e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Esau's one meal", "note": "Hebrews interprets Esau's sale of the birthright for one meal as profane conduct, making the Genesis choice an ethical reading relevant to the commentary's sorting of choices."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e4747ac2e6018be78563", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "lost blessing cannot be recovered", "note": "Hebrews adds that Esau later seeks the blessing with tears but cannot reverse the outcome; this Christian reading stresses the lasting cost of choosing immediate appetite."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6079da599f37fc796cc0", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "lost inherited blessing", "note": "Hebrews adds that Esau later seeks the blessing with tears but cannot reverse the result, stressing the lasting cost of his short-term exchange."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a3ab6bccd611ff39a600", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.12.27", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "shaken things removed, unshaken remaining", "note": "What can be shaken is removed so that what cannot be shaken may remain, a close conceptual formulation of sifting away what does not endure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-435152834316ab108f46", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.14", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no lasting city here", "note": "Hebrews says believers have no lasting city here but seek the one to come; it is an exact spatial formulation of preferring the abiding future to present worldly life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2019471e280a7cfc2ddc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.13.14", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "no abiding city here", "note": "Believers have no abiding city here but seek the coming one, an especially close counterpart to the claim that the other life is what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b7800ef35bcf6a261a4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.10", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God not forgetting charitable service", "note": "God is not unjust so as to forget the work and love shown in serving the saints, turning 87:6's not-forgetting into the preservation of acts of care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f1e62718c530b7bd0f3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Heb.6.7", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "useful crop receiving blessing", "note": "Land that produces a useful crop for those who cultivate it receives blessing from God, setting fruitful vegetation on the accepted side of a moral agricultural image."}

