{"connection_id": "BC-bc1a610ed8f911e7a7ac", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.10", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rich person fades like flower", "note": "James says the rich person will pass away like a field flower; the image echoes the commentary’s pasture that becomes debris and exposes worldly status as transient."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f57bd1ae884f1a3bf016", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.10", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rich person / flower of grass", "note": "The rich person's humiliation is likened to a flower of grass that passes away, directly tying social wealth to the commentary's flowerlike worldly life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7bca6b5f4e6ff795566a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.11", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flower withers in heat", "note": "The next verse depicts the flower falling and its beauty perishing; it extends the specific fading-flower image against the permanence of 87:17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0470f248284dd1721c6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.11", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "scorched grass and fading riches", "note": "Sun and heat wither grass, drop its flower, and destroy its beauty; James applies that process to the rich person's pursuits, closely matching the meadow's discarded end."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ac7ea09694d4cc8fc4a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.25", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "not a forgetful hearer", "note": "The person who continues in the perfect law is not a forgetful hearer but an active doer and is blessed, a specific ethical contrast for the commentary's forgetting motif."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b9415d646a1db072a1e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.5", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "poor chosen as kingdom heirs", "note": "God chooses the poor in the world as rich in faith and heirs of the kingdom, reversing worldly rank while preserving the commentary's horizon of enduring inheritance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff22d8809056e85f536f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.5.2", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riches and clothing decay", "note": "James says riches have rotted and garments are moth-eaten; this concretely exposes the perishability of the goods preferred in 87:16."}
{"connection_id": "BC-362ea108fe5389f851b0", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.12.25", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life in this world versus eternal life", "note": "Jesus says the one who loves life in this world loses it, while the one who hates it keeps it for eternal life; this directly addresses the preference named in 87:16."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef75e992693d7aa5ce56", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.12.25", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "life in this world / eternal life", "note": "Loving one's life in this world loses it, while relinquishing it keeps it for eternal life; the verse makes the commentary's preference an existential choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ead45bc0ce6e8eb8e218", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.15.6", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "non-abiding branch withers and burns", "note": "A branch that does not remain in Christ is thrown outside, withers, and is gathered for fire; it joins abiding, vegetable waste, and adverse finality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11650f20de15b77dc533", "ref": "SBLGNT:John.6.27", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perishing food / remaining food", "note": "Jesus contrasts food that perishes with food remaining into eternal life, directly framing worldly provision against what abides."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2b2f8207bbd3e21ed62a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.10.42", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "chosen portion not taken away", "note": "Jesus says Mary chose the good portion that will not be taken from her; it combines choosing what is better with its permanence, as in 87:17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f1d96eb29c08d92d42e1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.20", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rich fool dies that night", "note": "God tells the prosperous barn-builder that his life is demanded that night; this exposes the fragility of the worldly accumulation preferred in 87:16."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6182c34fbac588d17af", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.20", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rich fool's stored goods", "note": "The rich fool's life is demanded on the night he relies on accumulated goods, so his planned abundance cannot secure a remaining possession."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d8a98b891a9a7c312874", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.21", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "not rich toward God", "note": "Jesus concludes that the one who stores treasure for himself but is not rich toward God is a fool; the contrast gives moral content to what counts as good wealth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-888313fcb6969d87293d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.21", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rich toward God", "note": "The parable's conclusion contrasts laying up treasure for oneself with being rich toward God, a moral qualification of what counts as valuable wealth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a528bd05a995a8ce23d8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.12.33", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "imperishable treasure through giving", "note": "Jesus tells disciples to sell possessions and give alms, storing unfailing treasure in heaven; it joins the commentary’s selfless preference for others to its better, more lasting good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76a3ad7e7c3e68710c98", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.14.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "invite those unable to repay", "note": "Jesus says to invite the poor, disabled, lame, and blind rather than repayable guests; this enacts the commentary’s preference for another over oneself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54e0ccda6bb5d867eb6e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.14.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "feast for those unable to repay", "note": "Jesus tells a host to invite poor, crippled, lame, and blind people rather than reciprocal guests, a practical enactment of preferring another's need over self-advantage."}

