{"connection_id": "BC-c2fb590b9b9d15cfe2e3", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.46", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eternal punishment and life", "note": "The separated groups go to eternal punishment or eternal life; it gives concrete lasting consequences to the selection scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-496cb1f31c5b149a0209", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.25.46", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eternal punishment / eternal life", "note": "The final separation explicitly pairs eternal punishment with eternal life, the same warning that permanence can belong to punishment as well as blessedness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e8b3940af5b47294f32", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.3.12", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wheat gathered, chaff burned", "note": "John’s winnowing image gathers wheat into the barn and burns chaff; it directly joins the commentary’s debris image to a separation of lasting good from refuse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5b526e3c4e4b803d745f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.3.12", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wheat gathered and chaff burned", "note": "The coming judge gathers wheat into the barn and burns chaff with unquenchable fire, combining selected produce, discarded plant matter, and lasting adverse judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47b39fd06d644f254573", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.19", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasures decay on earth", "note": "Jesus warns that earthly treasure is vulnerable to moth and corrosion; this explains why the life and goods preferred in 87:16 cannot be the abiding good of 87:17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-12d32443e20ca50afbb4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.19", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earthly treasure's decay", "note": "Treasure on earth is subject to moth, corrosion, and theft, concretizing the commentary's claim that the worldly side is not what lasts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c841dd219041db73a40e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.20", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure in heaven", "note": "Jesus instead directs disciples to treasure in heaven where it is not destroyed; this supplies a specific enduring alternative to earthly preference."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a2a3b126789f206f9a7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.20", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "treasure in heaven", "note": "Treasure in heaven escapes moth, corrosion, and theft, giving a durable alternative to the perishable possessions of the preceding verse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-958c4f9a7a833d84e5c4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.24", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God and wealth as rival masters", "note": "No one can serve God and wealth because divided service produces love for one and contempt for the other; this is an explicit account of preference as allegiance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7d39bd4194c3f9c93ff", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.30", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "field grass valued despite fading", "note": "Jesus says God clothes grass that is alive today and thrown into the oven tomorrow; unlike the commentary’s use of spent pasture as a figure of the low lot, the image argues that even transient grass is cared for and grounds trust."}
{"connection_id": "BC-667f039df914d572e172", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.6.30", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "grass today and oven tomorrow", "note": "God clothes field grass that exists today and is put into an oven tomorrow; the image closely accompanies the commentary's grass, flower, and rapidly discarded vegetation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6044cfe41a5c8d088433", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "broad road ends in destruction", "note": "Jesus sets a broad way leading to destruction against a narrow way; this makes the commentary’s choice between the lower and better lot a concrete decision with opposed ends."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a20079f516874e02c706", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.14", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "narrow way leads to life", "note": "The narrow way leads to life and is found by few; it adds the positive outcome to the two-way choice that 87:16–17 presents."}
{"connection_id": "BC-61174ca0320b9bb0e4e7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.1.23", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "departing with Christ far better", "note": "Paul desires to depart and be with Christ because that is far better; it directly values the future with Christ above present life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5646d207e6c96dead9c2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.1.24", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remaining for others’ sake", "note": "Paul says staying in the flesh is more necessary for the Philippians; read beside his desire to depart, this shows present life chosen for service to others rather than its own worldly advantage."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7e03a4623868c44ed6ca", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.3", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "others counted more significant", "note": "Paul tells believers in humility to count others more significant than themselves; this is a concrete ethical use of the preference verb’s sense of putting another ahead of oneself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7eccea81917913173475", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.3", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "regarding others above oneself", "note": "Humility requires regarding others as surpassing oneself, directly corresponding to the commentary's explanation of preferring another over oneself."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7d6b7ec48e2761dbfb2", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.4", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "look to others’ interests", "note": "Paul says not to look only to one’s own interests but also to those of others; it completes the commentary’s sense of īthār as preferring another over self."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d87667dc90dad6c7337d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.2.4", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "others' interests", "note": "Each person is told to attend to others' interests as well as personal interests, supplying a concrete ethical form for right-directed preference."}
{"connection_id": "BC-903f4dd27179e2834c57", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.19", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earthly-minded end in destruction", "note": "Those whose minds are set on earthly things have destruction as their end, with appetite as god and shame as glory; it supplies a concrete New Testament diagnosis of worldly preference."}

