{"connection_id": "BC-cebc27ea37d44d59ef1a", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.20", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "citizenship in heaven", "note": "Believers' citizenship is in heaven and they await a savior from there, providing the positive location corresponding to the rejection of merely earthly-mindedness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f2c31b9038235963066e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Phil.3.8", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all things counted as rubbish", "note": "Paul counts all his former gains as loss and rubbish compared with gaining Christ; this makes the commentary’s debris image a deliberate revaluation of worldly status and profit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-674a929e668aa595d419", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.14.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "deeds following the dead", "note": "Those who die in the Lord rest from labor and their deeds follow them, specifying a form of good that survives the end of earthly life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-68d0d276d08e1ae5ad69", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.3.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "self-styled rich who are truly poor", "note": "The wealthy self-assessment is exposed as wretched, poor, blind, and naked; it overturns worldly measures of value in the commentary's register of high and low."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c23a207cf90773de8f25", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.11.5", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remnant by gracious election", "note": "Paul describes a remnant according to grace’s election; the verse joins the commentary’s retained bakiyya to its image of selecting the worthy lot."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37f1a179f5c470c08afd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.11.5", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "remnant chosen by grace", "note": "Paul reads Israel's survival through the Elijah narrative as a remnant according to gracious election, giving a Christian interpretation of the morally preserved remainder."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ed5c75f7bbcb7643b590", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.7", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perseverance seeking immortality", "note": "Those who persevere in good work and seek glory, honor, and incorruptibility receive eternal life, articulating a pursuit of lasting good through action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f44ce1c2165293f49b6", "ref": "Shepherd of Hermas, Similitude 1", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "foreign city and one's future city", "note": "Hermas treats believers as residents of a foreign city who should not build their hopes there because their proper city lies elsewhere; it develops the present-world versus lasting-destination image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-175037f4b60d73d43fad", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:14", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "death accepted for resurrection", "note": "A brother says he prefers death at human hands while relying on God to raise him; his choice under pressure resembles the commentary’s contrast of temporal life and a better lasting future."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a6e95216dfa88ebbaf19", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:36", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "short pain, ever-flowing life", "note": "The surviving brother contrasts brief suffering with the brothers’ share in ever-flowing life; it makes the temporal-versus-abiding comparison explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4fba0a7a24463dceff90", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:36", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "brief suffering / everlasting life", "note": "The youngest brother contrasts his brothers' short suffering and God's everlasting-life covenant with the king's coming punishment, sharpening the commentary's temporal-versus-lasting judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3862bc109cb411472179", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:9", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "resurrection after martyrdom", "note": "One son refuses the king’s terms and trusts the King of the universe to raise him to renewed life; fidelity is chosen over a threatened present life for enduring life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20286fbe88715ea0e3b1", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:9", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "king's torture / everlasting renewal", "note": "A tortured brother tells the king that the King of the universe will raise them to everlasting renewed life; it is a choice under a human tyrant for an enduring divine future."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1867c8c3095c1737cc11", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 2:7", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "possessions and worry / Torah and life to come", "note": "Hillel contrasts increased possessions and worry with acquiring Torah and life in the world to come, giving an interpretive hierarchy for worldly accumulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc89fd4e4be104689ed2", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:1", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "true wealth as contentment", "note": "Avot asks who is rich and answers: one who rejoices in his portion; this interprets wealth through contentment rather than the commentary’s lexicon note about material abundance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7eec36bbfcf61bd76b96", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "world as antechamber", "note": "Avot calls this world an antechamber before the world to come and urges preparation for the banquet hall; it makes the commentary’s worldly/afterlife contrast a practical choice of where to prepare."}
{"connection_id": "BC-478da9364e5bd929f408", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "corridor and banquet hall", "note": "This world is a vestibule before the world to come, where one must prepare to enter the banquet hall; it turns present life into a passage rather than the preferred destination."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8bb445b220cd7042f33a", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "better hour in world to come", "note": "Avot says an hour of bliss in the world to come is better than the whole life of this world; this directly states the comparison in 87:16–17, while also valuing repentance and good deeds in this life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8782958fb006551c437b", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "world to come's bliss", "note": "The maxim calls one hour of bliss in the world to come better than all life in this world, an explicit analogue to 87:17's comparison."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d0c5072c652ae530e839", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "repentance and deeds in this world", "note": "The first half of the same maxim says an hour of repentance and good deeds here is better than all the coming world; it complicates the commentary by valuing this life as the place of moral action."}

