{"connection_id": "BC-9ebf1f829719dbd3b702", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word enduring forever", "note": "After grass and flower fade, Isaiah says God’s word stands forever; beside 87:6’s promised recitation and 87:17’s abiding good, the contrast joins fading growth to enduring revelation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-253d9ba4963a688fe753", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "word standing forever", "note": "Against fading grass and flower, God's word stands forever, supplying the lasting side needed by the commentary's vegetation-and-abiding contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d6b2a9278caa1755852c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.51.6", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "earth wears out, salvation endures", "note": "Isaiah says earth and heavens pass while God’s salvation and righteousness endure; it closely frames the commentary’s contrast between transient dunya and what lasts."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2359e5a0d1f10965ce75", "ref": "WLC:Isa.51.6", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perishing creation / everlasting salvation", "note": "Heavens vanish, earth wears out, and inhabitants die, while God's salvation is forever and righteousness is not shattered; it makes the temporal/enduring comparison cosmic."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3030f69da2f1dfe1368c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.55.2", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "money for what does not satisfy", "note": "Isaiah asks why money and labor are spent on what does not satisfy, then calls listeners to eat what is good; it closely fits the commentary's scrutiny of valuable provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87ded466930460751645", "ref": "WLC:Isa.57.15", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "high God with lowly people", "note": "The high and exalted God also dwells with the crushed and lowly to revive them, challenging any simple equation of low status with refuse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb8a26e6b2d10dde1ccd", "ref": "WLC:Jer.17.11", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riches not gained justly", "note": "The person who gets riches unjustly leaves them in midlife and ends as a fool, combining unclean source with wealth's failure to remain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-244228eae62609328aea", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.2", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "flower withering and shadow fleeing", "note": "A human comes forth like a flower and withers, fleeing like a shadow that does not remain, a close poetic companion to the commentary's flower and non-abiding world."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cdd286343750b467dd47", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.10", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fallen grapes left for poor and alien", "note": "Israel may not strip the vineyard or gather its fallen grapes, but must leave them for poor people and resident aliens; discarded produce becomes deliberate generosity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-772a6f15318a74c213fc", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.9", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "field edge reserved for the poor", "note": "The harvest is not to be cut to the edge, and gleanings are left for the poor and immigrant; this gives “what remains” an ethical use rather than the contemptuous sense of dregs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d2b3caa6daf73c65278", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.19", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked as burned stubble", "note": "In WLC numbering (English Malachi 4:1), the coming day makes the arrogant and evildoers stubble consumed by fire; it links discarded vegetation with the fate of the rejected."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f7de7aac99ef2393c306", "ref": "WLC:Prov.10.2", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked treasure / life-delivering righteousness", "note": "Treasures of wickedness do not profit, whereas righteousness delivers from death; the proverb makes source and moral quality decisive for what counts as beneficial wealth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0acc31ca039a9345e90c", "ref": "WLC:Prov.11.4", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "riches fail at judgment", "note": "Riches do not profit on the day of wrath, but righteousness delivers from death, pressing the commentary's contrast between present possession and ultimate outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-09b19453311102b04f3a", "ref": "WLC:Prov.13.11", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wealth gained hastily dwindles", "note": "Proverbs says wealth acquired by vaporous means dwindles while gradual gathering grows; this adds a concrete warning about the source and durability of the wealth discussed in the commentary’s khayr gloss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-208ad9e07b7785636358", "ref": "WLC:Prov.13.11", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hasty wealth dwindling", "note": "Wealth gained hastily dwindles, while gradual gathering increases it, adding a concrete temporal test to the commentary's concern for rightly qualified wealth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c34c23b328ccc05c21e", "ref": "WLC:Prov.15.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "little with fear of God", "note": "Proverbs calls a little with fear of the LORD better than great treasure with turmoil; this gives the commentary’s “good” wealth a moral condition and contests abundance as the measure of worth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d4452841d54e88157bbe", "ref": "WLC:Prov.15.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "little with fear / treasure with turmoil", "note": "A little with fear of the LORD is better than great treasure with turmoil, comparing material plenty with the quality of the life that holds it."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-3c1f589a90e2434e9b8b", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.19", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "lowly with the poor preferred", "note": "Proverbs says it is better to be lowly among the poor than share spoil with the proud; this reverses the commentary’s low/dunyā pole by making low social position preferable to proud worldly gain."}
{"connection_id": "BC-764e1d75a26d13cc3dd0", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.8", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "righteous little over unjust abundance", "note": "Proverbs says a little with righteousness is better than great revenues with injustice; it qualifies the commentary’s lexicon note that wealth merits “good” only when abundant and from a clean source by making integrity outweigh abundance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6895d63655a923203047", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.8", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "little righteousness / great unjust gain", "note": "The proverb judges a little with righteousness better than large income with injustice, qualifying the commentary's abundance criterion by making moral source decisive."}
{"connection_id": "BC-97e5dce5695d9e1439b2", "ref": "WLC:Prov.19.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "giving to poor as loan to God", "note": "The one gracious to the poor lends to the LORD, who repays the deed; it makes voluntary preference for a needy other an act with divine return."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8571adcffa7e8eec7669", "ref": "WLC:Prov.22.1", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "good name chosen over riches", "note": "A good name is to be chosen over great riches, and favor over silver and gold; this directly joins the commentary’s language of choosing to a reordering of material value."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7606782b6547b845edef", "ref": "WLC:Prov.22.1", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "good name over riches", "note": "A good name is chosen over great riches and favor over silver and gold, directly depicting selection by worth rather than mere material quantity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b4682ee879fdc4a41265", "ref": "WLC:Prov.23.18", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew אַחֲרִית, future", "note": "The commentary’s form ٱلْءَاخِرَة (al-ākhirah) and Hebrew אַחֲרִית (ʾaḥarit), “future/outcome,” reflect the cognate Semitic root ʾ-ḥ-r/ʾ-kh-r; Proverbs uses it for the wise person’s hoped-for future, not unambiguously for a postmortem realm."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b3d1fff77a9c8fbb74f", "ref": "WLC:Prov.23.5", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wealth growing wings", "note": "When one fixes one's eyes on wealth, it is gone and flies heavenward like an eagle; it is a concrete warning against treating visible wealth as what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-66dee772211efb0d0e64", "ref": "WLC:Prov.30.9", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fullness, denial, and God's name", "note": "Agur fears that being full will lead him to deny God, giving the commentary's question of worldly wealth a particular spiritual danger."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c2c7510f58dc656bb5eb", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree that does not wither", "note": "The commentary places 87:5’s spent pasture opposite 87:17’s good and abiding lot; Psalm 1’s planted tree bears fruit and its leaf does not wither."}
{"connection_id": "BC-89c1d58f3d767ffd7f4f", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.4", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked as chaff", "note": "Psalm 1 calls the wicked wind-driven chaff; that makes the commentary’s 87:5 debris a human moral image as well as dead vegetation, set against the righteous tree."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef8a3ea34ad7878546cf", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.4", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked as wind-driven chaff", "note": "The wicked are chaff that the wind drives away, a pointed ethical use of plant refuse beside the commentary's discarded ghutha' and lower-human comparison."}
{"connection_id": "BC-753556d3953b23861c32", "ref": "WLC:Ps.102.27", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "creation changes, God remains", "note": "The heavens and earth wear out and change, while God remains the same; this gives 87:17’s permanence a creation-scale contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4cd476c05481f128c271", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.15", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human life like grass", "note": "Human days are like grass and a field flower that passes; this echoes 87:5’s pasture turned to debris and the commentary’s low valuation of worldly life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-552944c09c949276e0bf", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.15", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "human days as grass", "note": "Human days are grass and a person's flourishing is a field flower, a direct parallel for the commentary's flower image of worldly life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9506884e9fe8273d9065", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "gone and no longer known", "note": "After the wind passes, the flower is gone and its place knows it no more; this adds a specific fading-and-forgetting counterpart to the commentary's forgotten residue."}
{"connection_id": "BC-63685964f3fec7a5c1e6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "steadfast love everlasting", "note": "Against the human flower that fades, the next verse says God’s steadfast love is everlasting; it supplies a specific enduring counterpart to the commentary’s 87:17."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a859746c7b88e6e0c5e4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.103.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "enduring steadfast love", "note": "The LORD's steadfast love is from everlasting to everlasting, giving the psalm's answer to its prior grass-and-flower transience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f5aa94403c1640e859e9", "ref": "WLC:Ps.113.7", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "poor raised from dust and ash heap", "note": "Rather than leaving the lowly among refuse, the psalm says God raises the poor from dust and lifts the needy from the ash heap; it reverses the commentary's social application of debris."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c24a2b69530c2cfd782d", "ref": "WLC:Ps.119.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "does not forget the word", "note": "The psalmist delights in God’s statutes and says he will not forget the word; it gives a human devotional counterpart to 87:6’s promised retention of recitation."}

