{"connection_id": "BC-f046dbc1527135251b6a", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "little of righteous better", "note": "The psalm says the righteous person’s little is better than the abundance of many wicked; it joins “better” to clean moral source while reversing the lexicon’s emphasis on plenty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eaaca645e3ad2437bcc4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "little righteous / abundance wicked", "note": "The small portion of the righteous is better than the abundance of many wicked, another direct reversal of an amount-based account of good wealth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-519f52a67c1876470d64", "ref": "WLC:Ps.49.17", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wealth left at death", "note": "The rich person takes nothing when he dies; the verse sharply limits the value of the worldly goods preferred in 87:16."}
{"connection_id": "BC-867a428cabefde828d57", "ref": "WLC:Ps.73.25", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "desire in heaven and earth", "note": "The psalmist says there is none in heaven besides God and nothing on earth he desires; this is a concrete preference for God over earthly life and goods."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f18e5c0dda61adc11fe", "ref": "WLC:Ps.73.26", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God as lasting portion", "note": "When flesh and heart fail, God remains the psalmist’s portion forever; that makes 87:17’s better, more lasting share a personal trust rather than a material asset."}
{"connection_id": "BC-48100f41b32a77991f35", "ref": "WLC:Ruth.2.16", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "bundled grain deliberately left", "note": "Boaz orders workers to pull grain from bundles and leave it for Ruth to glean without rebuke, a narrative instance of turning surplus and leftovers toward a migrant widow's need."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e805766bfd552faa434", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.3.12", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "humble remnant seeks refuge", "note": "Zephaniah says God leaves a humble and lowly people who take refuge in him; this unites the commentary’s “low” valuation and its bakiyya that still has religious substance, reversing any equation of low social standing with refuse."}
{"connection_id": "BC-21216602670fbcd4a2ed", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.3.12", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "humble and lowly remnant", "note": "God leaves a humble, lowly people who seek refuge in his name, joining low status to the positive remnant rather than to discard."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db7d61109442f579b1da", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.3.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remnant does no injustice", "note": "The remnant of Israel speaks no lies and does no injustice; this makes the commentary’s morally intact remainder concrete, rather than treating what remains as mere debris."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74787dc6dc1c2e3cd23b", "ref": "WLC:Zeph.3.13", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remnant without wrong or deceit", "note": "Israel's remnant does no wrong and speaks no lies; it gives the commentary's “remaining ones” a concrete moral profile."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d2822822c949919450a", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 3:1", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "souls held by God", "note": "The souls of the righteous are in God’s hand and are at peace despite seeming lost; this answers the commentary’s debris/forgetting side with what remains secure before God."}
{"connection_id": "BC-25a8ee1ac016c2aa7a74", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 3:4", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hope of immortality", "note": "Though the righteous seem punished and dead, their hope is full of immortality; this gives 87:17’s lasting good a specific postmortem sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0f33182b2ed96ae77323", "ref": "Wisdom of Solomon 3:4", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hope full of immortality", "note": "The righteous may appear punished to observers, yet their hope is full of immortality, making present appearance a poor measure of lasting worth."}
{"connection_id": "BC-101c9168610c45243b78", "ref": "b. Eruvin 22a", "target": "sec16", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "today for doing, tomorrow for reward", "note": "The talmudic reading of the commandment says this day is for performing them and the next for receiving reward, making present moral action and future recompense distinct without making present life the final good."}

