 {"connection_id":"BC-60534ab838ebf34da6f6","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.19.21","status":"rejected","reason":"This sale-and-give instruction overlaps the selected Luke 12:33 passage; the narrative's demand to follow Jesus is beyond the paragraph's point.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.19.21"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-aafcfdf1b44d22de67f4","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.19.22","status":"rejected","reason":"The wealthy inquirer grieves and leaves, but this verse itself does not say he chose worldly life over eternal life.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.19.22"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-645e3d54403ba0842c64","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.32","status":"rejected","reason":"Sheep and goats are separated, but the agricultural chaff image in Matthew 3:12 fits the paragraph's refuse more closely.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.25.32"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-dcc90f8af66672d1d730","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.40","status":"accepted","reason":"The least are identified with the king, making care for low-status people valuable rather than discardable.","paragraphs":[62],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Jas.2.5","SBLGNT:1Cor.1.28","SBLGNT:Matt.25.40"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-b3aad0f96c2b45f2dd68","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.40","status":"accepted","reason":"The Greek counts action toward one of the least as action toward the king, reversing social-value assumptions.","paragraphs":[62],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Jas.2.5","SBLGNT:1Cor.1.28","SBLGNT:Matt.25.40"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-005"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c2fb590b9b9d15cfe2e3","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.46","status":"rejected","reason":"Eternal punishment and eternal life are explicit, but Daniel 12:2 gives the paired outcomes within the Hebrew-scriptural comparison already annotated.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.25.46"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-496cb1f31c5b149a0209","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.25.46","status":"rejected","reason":"Both eternal destinies appear, but this adds no distinct observation beyond the Daniel 12:2 dual outcome.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.25.46"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2e8b3940af5b47294f32","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.3.12","status":"accepted","reason":"Wheat is gathered and chaff burned; plant refuse becomes eschatological sorting.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.3.12","SBLGNT:Luke.3.17","SBLGNT:Heb.6.7","SBLGNT:Heb.6.8"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-5b526e3c4e4b803d745f","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.3.12","status":"accepted","reason":"The Greek specifically contrasts stored wheat and chaff consumed in unquenchable fire, matching the paragraph's selected/refuse sides.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.3.12","SBLGNT:Luke.3.17","SBLGNT:Heb.6.7","SBLGNT:Heb.6.8"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-47b39fd06d644f254573","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.6.19","status":"accepted","reason":"Earthly treasure decays and can be stolen; verse 20 supplies the contrasting heavenly treasure.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.6.19","SBLGNT:Matt.6.20","SBLGNT:Luke.12.33","SBLGNT:1Tim.6.17","SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-12d32443e20ca50afbb4","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.6.19","status":"accepted","reason":"Moth, decay and theft are concrete tests of worldly wealth's lack of permanence.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.6.19","SBLGNT:Matt.6.20","SBLGNT:Luke.12.33","SBLGNT:1Tim.6.17","SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c841dd219041db73a40e","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.6.20","status":"accepted","reason":"Heavenly treasure is explicitly protected from the decay and loss just named on earth.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.6.19","SBLGNT:Matt.6.20","SBLGNT:Luke.12.33","SBLGNT:1Tim.6.17","SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6a2a3b126789f206f9a7","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.6.20","status":"accepted","reason":"The text gives a lasting alternative to perishable earthly stores in the same treasure comparison.","paragraphs":[61],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.6.19","SBLGNT:Matt.6.20","SBLGNT:Luke.12.33","SBLGNT:1Tim.6.17","SBLGNT:1Tim.6.19"],"annotations":["S087-INC-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-958c4f9a7a833d84e5c4","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.6.24","status":"rejected","reason":"God versus wealth as rival masters is a real allegiance distinction, but the verse does not state the better or abiding quality central here.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.6.24"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f7d39bd4194c3f9c93ff","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.6.30","status":"rejected","reason":"Grass thrown in the oven grounds trust in God's care here, not an afterlife sorting; using it as disposable people would invert Jesus' point.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.6.30"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-667f039df914d572e172","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.6.30","status":"rejected","reason":"The short-lived field grass is clothed by God, so the verse's point is provision despite transience rather than rejection of worldly life.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.6.30"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6044cfe41a5c8d088433","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.7.13","status":"rejected","reason":"The broad road's destruction is an ethical two-way image, but it lacks the specific worldly/enduring terms of 87:16-17.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.7.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a20079f516874e02c706","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.7.14","status":"rejected","reason":"The narrow road leads to life, but choosing a route adds a different spatial metaphor without the paragraph's permanence contrast.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.7.14"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-61174ca0320b9bb0e4e7","ref":"SBLGNT:Phil.1.23","status":"rejected","reason":"Paul calls departing to Christ better, but the christological personal desire is less close to the section's general comparison than Hebrews 10:34.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Phil.1.23"],"annotations":[]}
*** End Patch"));

