text(await tools.apply_patch("*** Begin Patch
*** Update File: /Volumes/aro/projects/prose_generation/enrichment/bible/work/s087/ehlikitap-images.sol.max.session-20261005/sec16/verdicts.jsonl
@@
 {"connection_id":"BC-f4ecb78263b9b0b3022d","ref":"WLC:Dan.12.2","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew explicitly pairs life and contempt each qualified as everlasting, supporting the section's adverse-permanence note.","paragraphs":[64],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.12.2","WLC:Eccl.3.21","WLC:Eccl.9.5"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-008"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f313d647f561c27cb23d","ref":"WLC:Dan.3.18","status":"accepted","reason":"The three officials refuse the king's image regardless of rescue by God; divine allegiance outranks royal threat.","paragraphs":[64],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.17","WLC:Dan.3.17","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-34204e297fa3490ce6c7","ref":"WLC:Dan.3.18","status":"accepted","reason":"The Aramaic text explicitly refuses worship of the king's gods and image under threat, without promising afterlife reward.","paragraphs":[64],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.17","WLC:Dan.3.17","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-dc4dfda34837825b3c91","ref":"WLC:Deut.24.19","status":"accepted","reason":"An accidentally forgotten sheaf becomes the protected portion of alien, orphan and widow, reversing refuse valuation.","paragraphs":[62],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.24.19","WLC:Lev.19.9","WLC:Lev.19.10","WLC:Ruth.2.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4dd421d68bb4f2c0d5e3","ref":"WLC:Deut.24.19","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew says not to return for the forgotten sheaf because it belongs to vulnerable people; paragraph 62 expresses that reversal.","paragraphs":[62],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.24.19","WLC:Lev.19.9","WLC:Lev.19.10","WLC:Ruth.2.16"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-003"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-acb5005582ad4dc987fa","ref":"WLC:Deut.30.15","status":"rejected","reason":"Life/good versus death/evil is a covenantal choice, but it does not distinguish worldly from afterlife or lasting from refuse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.30.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-8b9786523e4abfd9e212","ref":"WLC:Deut.30.19","status":"rejected","reason":"Choose life is explicit, yet the context is continued life for Israel and descendants in the land, not the afterlife of 87:17.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.30.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-39317d63163c695d116a","ref":"WLC:Deut.30.19","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse's choice of covenantal life is not the two-world valuation here; equating them would flatten distinct horizons.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.30.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-cc17eb5644add4aeb499","ref":"WLC:Deut.31.19","status":"rejected","reason":"The song is written and taught to Israel, but the present section's paragraph 63 mentions forgetting only as part of a selection image, not as a recitation claim.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.31.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a07dc394aa45fcf6f446","ref":"WLC:Deut.31.21","status":"rejected","reason":"The song not being forgotten from descendants' mouths concerns communal memory; 87:6 promises the prophet's own recitation, a different subject.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.31.21"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-5c1fdaa2c2bbfd66f6a1","ref":"WLC:Deut.32.15","status":"rejected","reason":"Jeshurun's prosperity and apostasy illuminate wealth's danger but add no explicit enduring alternative to the section's preference.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.32.15"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9187950d6aa3f759c3dc","ref":"WLC:Deut.7.7","status":"rejected","reason":"Israel's choice despite small numbers shows election independent of quantity, but the choice is God's national election rather than a person's worldly preference.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.7.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-4f1c2c8a6ae798253c82","ref":"WLC:Deut.8.14","status":"rejected","reason":"Prosperity may make Israel forget God, but forgetting here is religious memory, unlike the section's discarded debris and lasting choice.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Deut.8.14"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-059edacc8932688e0b0c","ref":"WLC:Eccl.12.7","status":"rejected","reason":"Dust and spirit return to their sources, but the verse does not rate an afterlife as better or describe a moral selection.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Eccl.12.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-e165e4b87326005986a5","ref":"WLC:Eccl.3.19","status":"rejected","reason":"Human and animal mortality is a sober countervoice, but the proposed claim that it rules out distinct postmortem destinies exceeds the verse; 3:21 more clearly marks uncertainty.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Eccl.3.19"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9e90b4a8b8e84a916400","ref":"WLC:Eccl.3.21","status":"accepted","reason":"Qoheleth explicitly asks who knows the human spirit's course, a check on imposing a uniform afterlife doctrine on Hebrew texts.","paragraphs":[64],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.12.2","WLC:Eccl.3.21","WLC:Eccl.9.5"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-008"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-c7fce8c86515475abf05","ref":"WLC:Eccl.9.5","status":"accepted","reason":"The verse says the dead know nothing and their memory is forgotten; the annotation preserves this different register without calling it a total denial of afterlife.","paragraphs":[64],"evidence":["WLC:Dan.12.2","WLC:Eccl.3.21","WLC:Eccl.9.5"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-008"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-480596c664fca6ffc613","ref":"WLC:Eccl.9.9","status":"rejected","reason":"Enjoying one's allotted days is present-life counsel, but it does not itself deny a future life or instruct preferring the world over God.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Eccl.9.9"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-16a503c04f19f1de1a7e","ref":"WLC:Exod.1.17","status":"accepted","reason":"The midwives fear God and disobey Pharaoh's lethal order, a direct royal-threat choice in the Mosaic setting.","paragraphs":[64],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.17","WLC:Dan.3.17","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-590aa83a32f4d9b3c237","ref":"WLC:Exod.1.17","status":"accepted","reason":"Fear of God motivates refusal of Pharaoh and preserves the children; paragraph 64 compares this specific allegiance.","paragraphs":[64],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.17","WLC:Dan.3.17","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
*** End Patch"));

