text(await tools.apply_patch("*** Begin Patch
*** Update File: /Volumes/aro/projects/prose_generation/enrichment/bible/work/s087/ehlikitap-images.sol.max.session-20261005/sec16/verdicts.jsonl
@@
 {"connection_id":"BC-590aa83a32f4d9b3c237","ref":"WLC:Exod.1.17","status":"accepted","reason":"Fear of God motivates refusal of Pharaoh and preserves the children; paragraph 64 compares this specific allegiance.","paragraphs":[64],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.1.17","WLC:Dan.3.17","WLC:Dan.3.18"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-007"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fdef47e658ae4614a087","ref":"WLC:Exod.8.23","status":"rejected","reason":"WLC Exodus 8:23 is Moses asking for a three-day desert journey, not the magicians' 'finger of God'; that scene is WLC 8:15.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Exod.8.23"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fc37af90d68eecc70c8e","ref":"WLC:Ezek.22.18","status":"rejected","reason":"The verse does call Israel dross, but its furnace is a judgment on collective corruption, not a hierarchy of socially low people or world/afterlife goods.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Ezek.22.18"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-6e695ef2126bf412a29a","ref":"WLC:Gen.25.34","status":"rejected","reason":"Esau despises his birthright after eating, but the inheritance is a family right, not an expressly abiding afterlife good.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.25.34"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ec2324a9aa7abc30f168","ref":"WLC:Gen.25.34","status":"rejected","reason":"The one-meal exchange illustrates short-term valuation but lacks the explicit eternal horizon of Hebrews 10:34 and the Moses reading.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.25.34"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9d31b082638ba519d5c5","ref":"WLC:Gen.41.51","status":"unresolved","reason":"The WLC has inflected נַשַּׁנִי, not the cited bare נשה; a cognacy claim with Arabic نسي needs a checked comparative lexicon beyond this verse.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.41.51"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-75dfc39e3a055dbf9ad0","ref":"WLC:Gen.41.51","status":"unresolved","reason":"Joseph's forgetting hardship is verified, but the Hebrew נַשַּׁנִי to Arabic nasiya sound relation is not established from local verse evidence.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.41.51"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1bef01c896c5686ccd7f","ref":"WLC:Gen.45.7","status":"rejected","reason":"Joseph preserves a remnant of his family, but the verse says God sent him before them, not that he was preferred over his brothers; Zephaniah states moral remnant more closely.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Gen.45.7"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-a1d75345df4fc8b3f866","ref":"WLC:Isa.1.25","status":"rejected","reason":"Refining Zion's dross concerns purification of a city, a different process from choosing transient or enduring goods in these paragraphs.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.1.25"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1d7258be2d0ee0328877","ref":"WLC:Isa.10.20","status":"rejected","reason":"The remnant ceases relying on its oppressor and relies on God, a useful allegiance detail already more concretely shown by the selected Zephaniah and Amos remnant texts.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.10.20"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f14013dbf61338766b30","ref":"WLC:Isa.37.31","status":"rejected","reason":"Judah's remnant rooting and fruiting is a restoration metaphor, but this remnant's moral profile is not given here as in Zephaniah 3:13.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.37.31"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-ed26ca78bd781eff5458","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.6","status":"accepted","reason":"All flesh is grass and its beauty a field flower, beginning the passage's fading-versus-enduring sequence.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.6","WLC:Isa.40.7","WLC:Isa.40.8","WLC:Ps.103.15","WLC:Ps.103.16","WLC:Ps.103.17"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-0c87865d44da1690c45e","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.7","status":"accepted","reason":"The grass withers and flower fades under the LORD's breath, connecting the plant image to human transience.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.6","WLC:Isa.40.7","WLC:Isa.40.8","WLC:Ps.103.15","WLC:Ps.103.16","WLC:Ps.103.17"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-9ebf1f829719dbd3b702","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.8","status":"accepted","reason":"God's word stands forever against the withered grass, supplying the lasting side of the image sequence.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.6","WLC:Isa.40.7","WLC:Isa.40.8","WLC:Ps.103.15","WLC:Ps.103.16","WLC:Ps.103.17"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-253d9ba4963a688fe753","ref":"WLC:Isa.40.8","status":"accepted","reason":"The Hebrew explicitly juxtaposes fading grass and flower with the divine word standing forever.","paragraphs":[63],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.40.6","WLC:Isa.40.7","WLC:Isa.40.8","WLC:Ps.103.15","WLC:Ps.103.16","WLC:Ps.103.17"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-006"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-d6b2a9278caa1755852c","ref":"WLC:Isa.51.6","status":"rejected","reason":"Creation wears out while salvation endures, a valid cosmic form of the contrast; Isaiah 40:6-8 retains the section's grass and flower wording.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.51.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-2359e5a0d1f10965ce75","ref":"WLC:Isa.51.6","status":"rejected","reason":"This cosmic fading/salvation pair lacks the vegetation and selective sorting that organize paragraph 63; Isaiah 40 is more precise.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.51.6"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3030f69da2f1dfe1368c","ref":"WLC:Isa.55.2","status":"rejected","reason":"Spending on what does not satisfy probes value, but the verse concerns food and listening rather than finite versus abiding life.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Isa.55.2"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-87ded466930460751645","ref":"WLC:Isa.57.15","status":"accepted","reason":"The high and holy God dwells with lowly and crushed people to revive them, blocking social-lowliness-as-refuse inference.","paragraphs":[62],"evidence":["WLC:Ps.113.7","WLC:Isa.57.15","WLC:Prov.16.19"],"annotations":["S087-TEV-MTF-004"]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fb8a26e6b2d10dde1ccd","ref":"WLC:Jer.17.11","status":"rejected","reason":"Unjust wealth is left in midlife, but the verse's bird proverb does not state a better lasting alternative or clean abundance criterion.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["WLC:Jer.17.11"],"annotations":[]}
*** End Patch"));

